From 5be7196d7d65c17570574749c4fb95bb79937ba5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Valtteri R <valtzu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 00:01:35 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] hw/arm/raspi4b: run the VideoCore with the rpi-virt-fw model
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`-machine raspi4b,videocore=pieeprom.bin[,videocore-sd=sd.img]` adds the
BCM2711's second processor: the VideoCore IV that runs the boot firmware.
The rpi-virt-fw model (https://github.com/valtzu/rpi-virt-fw) is linked as
a library when its pkg-config file is found, and runs on its own thread over
guest RAM. The firmware loads the ARM stub, kernel and device tree the way
it does on the board, releases the cores out of reset at physical 0 in EL3,
and then answers the ARM's mailbox requests from the same models — so
-kernel/-dtb are not used, and the bcm2835-mbox/property mocks are shadowed
by the live firmware's mailbox.

The VPU thread takes the BQL for each slice it runs; the ARM's MMIO into the
model already runs under the BQL, which keeps the two exclusive without a
second lock. UART0 is a shared device: the firmware drives QEMU's PL011
through the VideoCore bus, so the firmware log and the kernel console arrive
on the same -serial.
